# Bloomly App - Cursor AI Rules

You are an expert in TypeScript, React Native, Expo, and Mobile UI development.

## Project Overview

- React Native mobile application using Expo
- File-based routing with expo-router
- NativeWind (Tailwind CSS) for styling
- TypeScript for type safety

## Code Style and Structure

- Write concise, technical TypeScript code with accurate examples
- Use functional and declarative programming patterns; avoid classes
- Prefer iteration and modularization over code duplication
- Use descriptive variable names with auxiliary verbs (e.g., isLoading, hasError)
- Structure files: exported component, subcomponents, helpers, static content, types
- 4 spaces for indentation
- No semicolons
- Single quotes for strings

## Naming Conventions

- Use lowercase with dashes for directories (e.g., components/auth-wizard)
- Favor named exports for components
- Use PascalCase for component file names
- Use camelCase for utility functions and hooks

## File Structure

- /app - Application screens (using expo-router file-based routing)
- /components - Reusable UI components
- /hooks - Custom React hooks
- /utils - Utility functions
- /types - TypeScript types and interfaces
- /assets - Static assets (images, fonts)
- /constants - App constants and configuration

## Production Safety (Pre-App Store Launch)

- **Scope confirmation:** Before making changes that affect more files or code than the immediate task, ask the user for confirmation. This includes: refactors spanning multiple components, navigation/routing changes beyond the target screen, store or hook changes that ripple across the app, and any modification touching 3+ files.
- **AudioPlayer close:** AudioPlayer X button ALWAYS navigates back to the previous screen. Entry points must use `router.push` (not `replace`) so the stack is preserved.

## Protected Paths

- **android/_backups/** – Pre-compression copy of assets. Safety net for image compression. Do not delete, and do not include in builds; it is excluded via .easignore.

## Pathway / timegate logic

- **Three doors after DateSelection (trial only):** Door 1 – Fresh (Trial 1) → ChakraHome → Waiting room. Door 2 – Trial 2 begins → ChakraHome → Waiting room. Door 3 – Both trials end → SimpleGraceTransition → paywall → ChakraHub. Lifetime users do not use these doors (WelcomeScreen → ChakraHub).
- **Sequence cards (trial-only):** SimpleGraceTransition, GraceOfThePresence, RestingBlessing, SealOfTheInitiate, JourneySummaryGift, CommitmentGate may only appear in the trial pathway. They must never be shown to Lifetime users; do not remove or bypass the `hasLifetimeAccess` check in `shouldShowCommitmentGate` (ChakraHome).

## Production: Firebase & Audio (Hero core, fail-safes)

- **Firebase Storage paths:** Single source of truth is `constants/firebaseStoragePaths.ts`. Hooks (useTuningForkAudio, useCrystalBowlAudio, useEmbodimentAudio, useAncestralWisdomAudio) must use these folder names; filenames stay in sync with Storage bucket (case-sensitive).
- **Firebase init:** Graceful when config missing (no crash). Rate limiting (`rateLimiter`), retry with backoff (`audioRetry`), and URL cache (`audioCache`) protect high-traffic and transient failures.
- **Audio playback – local-first (locked):** Once a file is on device (localUri or getLocalAudioUri), playback must use it only. Never stream from URL when local file exists. Implemented in `crystalBowlPlayback.ts` and `audioDownload.ts`; do not bypass. Audio should never cut off once downloaded.
- **Download/cache:** Cache dir is `FileSystem.cacheDirectory/audio/`. Full-file and "head" (first ~3 min) cache supported; long files use resumable download. Tuning fork / crystal bowl / embodiment hooks check local first, then Firebase URL.
- **APP1 vs APP2 audio (no overlap):** Trial (APP1) never sets `audioOrigin === "music-room"` – AudioLibrary redirects trial to ChakraHome, so only lifetime can use Frequency of Gnosis / setSourceWithPlaylist. Trial audio is always "other" (embodiment, SoundBath, Head to Heart from chakra day). When user is on full-screen AudioPlayer, only AudioPlayer owns the track; MusicRoomAudioManager and OtherOriginAudioManager must not play (they unload / skip init when pathname is AudioPlayer). Trial: no mini player (PermanentMenuBar returns null); on leaving AudioPlayer, reset() clears store. Lifetime: mini player for both origins; no reset on leave so playback can continue.
- **Full-player position (hard rule):** All full-screen Audio Player tracks MUST hold their place when the user leaves. Position is persisted on close (X or back) in `closePlayerAndNavigate` and restored when reopening the same track. No code path may reset full-player position without persisting first. Do not remove or bypass position persistence on close; do not add resets that clear position for full-player without saving it.

## Locked / Hero Designs (Do Not Redesign)

- **App icon (home screen):** Soul School app icon on the phone MUST always be black background with color wheel logo. Never white background. Single source in production: `assets/images/ChakraWheel_ONBLACK_300DPI.png`, set via `APP_ICON_BLACK` in `app.config.js` (icon, ios.icon, android.adaptiveIcon.foregroundImage). Do not replace with 7chakras.png or any white-background asset for the app icon.
- **Hero splash screen (SplashScreenReveal):** Must always use `SoulSchool_HERO_Logo.png`. Never replace it with `SoulSchool_Error_HeroLogo.png` or any other image. Single source: `components/SplashScreenReveal.tsx`.
- **Trials home chakra stack (Android):** The 7 chakra balls on the trials home screen must ALWAYS be pinned to the base (bottom) of the main phone screen. Layout is LOCKED: `IntegratedProgressStack.tsx` uses a viewport-height container with `justifyContent: "flex-end"` and `paddingBottom: TRIAL_HOME_ROOT_CHAKRA.BOTTOM_PADDING`; constants in `constants/layout.ts` (TRIAL_HOME_ROOT_CHAKRA). Do not center the stack; base-pinned placement is non-negotiable.
- **Goodbye pages (days 1–7):** `components/chakras/GoodbyeModal.tsx` is the single source for all goodbye/completion screens. Design is locked and mastered: top separator; day name (e.g. "Monday Root Day") in cormorant-regular label style; identity "I Am" and hero quote in hero affirmation style (cormorant-italic, 28px, 0.82 white); smaller chakra ball (88px); closing message in gentle cormorant-italic; thin bottom separator; bottom section with gold separator, gift card + spacing, "Open Your Gift", tomorrow preview line + time, Home button. No "Affirmation" label; no "Your chakra cards are collected in the Gallery" line. **Android build locked:** The Crown chakra goodbye screen (Sunday Crown Day) is the master design for all Android goodbye screens—exact style, placement, spacing, hero affirmation frame (slightly rounded corners, wider), padding between Open Your Gift and Home, and raised content. Do not change Android goodbye layout, typography, or section structure without explicit user request.

## TypeScript Usage

- Use TypeScript for all code
- Use both interfaces and types appropriately: interfaces for object shapes that might be extended, types for unions, intersections, and mapped types
- Prefer const objects with as const for string-based enumerations; consider TypeScript enums for numeric values when appropriate
- Use functional components with proper TypeScript typing
- Use strict mode in TypeScript for better type safety
- Export and reuse types when applicable

## Styling with NativeWind

- Prefer NativeWind (Tailwind CSS) with className prop for most styling needs
- Use StyleSheet.create for performance-critical components or when strong type safety is needed
- Use Tailwind's responsive prefixes (sm:, md:, lg:, xl:) for responsive layouts
- Define custom colors and theme extensions in tailwind.config.js
- Follow utility-first approach for component styling
- Group related styles logically in className strings
- Use gap-_ instead of space-y-_ and space-x-\* classes for better compatibility with Expo Go
- When creating lists of elements, use gap-\* on parent containers for consistent spacing
- Avoid using divide-\* classes as they don't work properly on mobile

## Navigation with Expo Router

- Use expo-router for file-based routing and navigation
- Follow the folder structure conventions (\_layout.tsx for layouts)
- Use Stack, Tabs, and other navigation components from expo-router
- Use dynamic routes with proper type safety
- Apply screenOptions consistently across related routes

## State Management

- Use Zustand for global application state
- Use TanStack Query for server state management and data fetching
- Keep state as local as possible; lift state only when necessary
- Use React Context sparingly for specific complex scenarios
- Use MMKV for efficient key-value storage instead of AsyncStorage

## Component Patterns

- Keep components small and focused on a single responsibility
- Use composition over inheritance
- Implement proper loading, error, and empty states for data-dependent components
- Use ErrorBoundary components to catch and handle UI errors
- Break down large components into smaller, reusable parts

## Form Handling

- Use react-hook-form for form management
- Implement proper validation with clear error messages
- Provide immediate feedback for form interactions
- Use controlled components where necessary
- Ensure proper keyboard handling for form inputs

## Lists and Performance

- Use LegendList for rendering lists instead of FlatList when possible
- Implement proper list virtualization for long lists
- Use proper key props for list items
- Optimize list rendering performance
- Implement pull-to-refresh and pagination where appropriate

## Images and Media

- Use ExpoImage for optimized image loading and caching
- Provide proper image dimensions to avoid layout shifts
- Implement progressive loading for large images
- Support various screen densities with appropriate image resolutions
- Use WebP format where supported for better compression

## Gestures and Animations

- Use react-native-gesture-handler for touch interactions
- Use react-native-reanimated for smooth, performant animations
- Keep animations subtle and purposeful
- Ensure animations don't interfere with usability
- Use shared element transitions for better UX where appropriate

## Authentication and User Management

